2806: to the constructor theorem rather than making them independent fixed-point
2807: theorems.
2808: \end{proof}
2809: 
2810: \begin{proposition}[Dependency normal form for the publication-safety chain]
2811: \label{prop:publication-safety-dependency-normal-form}
2812: Let $c$ be a load-bearing claim whose certificate
2813: \(\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)=\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) is constructed by
2814: the finite schema verifier from the submitted surface $S$, ledger $L$, and
2815: dispatch table $\Gamma$.  Then every use of the publication-safety interface in
2816: the main theorem chain factors through the following ordered dependency graph:
2817: \begin{enumerate}
2818: \item \emph{construction and schema-relative exactness}: the tuple
2819: \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) is formed from submitted finite records and is accepted
2820: exactly when clauses A1--A6 of
2821: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} hold, as proved by
2822: Theorems~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract} and
2823: \ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness};
2824: \item \emph{extension-budget necessity and sufficiency}: for any genuinely
2825: stronger reading $c^{+}$ that is not record-equivalent to $c$, acceptance of
2826: $c^{+}$ is equivalent to the presence of the finite extension record
2827: \(\Delta(c,c^{+})=(\Delta K,\Delta E,\Delta H,\Delta R,\Delta D,\Delta B)\) of
2828: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}; and
2829: \item \emph{maximality}: if the required nonempty extension coordinates are
2830: absent, the original certificate remains accepted for $c$ and is maximal below
2831: $c^{+}$ on the submitted finite surface, while $c^{+}$ is rejected by a finite
2832: bad subrecord as in Proposition~\ref{prop:publication-safety-maximality}.
2833: \end{enumerate}
2834: Consequently the sharp ``if and only if'' and maximality language in
2835: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} is only the composite of these
2836: three finite-record statements.  It is not a separate semantic assertion about
2837: gate implementations, Lean/BEDC source truth, artifact behaviour, daemon runs, or
2838: venue acceptance.
2839: \end{proposition}
2840: 
2841: \begin{proof}
2842: The first dependency is the construction part.  By
2843: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, the verifier input consists
2844: only of the submitted finite certificate fields, and its predicates are exactly
2845: the six checks A1--A6.  The schema-relative exactness theorem,
2846: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, proves the biconditional between
2847: passing those checks and satisfying the record-acceptance semantics for the
2848: dispatch table $\Gamma$.  Thus the publication-safety theorem can construct no
2849: certificate other than the tuple read from the finite surface, and acceptance of
2850: that tuple is exact only relative to the printed schema.
2851: 
2852: For the second dependency, let $c^{+}$ be a genuinely stronger reading.  If it is
2853: accepted, then it cannot be record-equivalent to $c$, so
2854: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} supplies at least one
2855: changed finite verifier coordinate.  Collecting the changed kind, evidence,
2856: human-boundary, gate, discovery, and command-boundary coordinates gives exactly
2857: the six-component extension record of
2858: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.  Conversely, if that
2859: extension record is present and its assigned gates pass, the enlarged tuple
2860: satisfies the corresponding clauses A1--A6, so schema-relative exactness accepts
2861: the stronger reading.  This proves both necessity and sufficiency of the
2862: extension budget.
2863: 
2864: The maximality dependency is then immediate.  If a genuine stronger reading has
2865: no paid extension coordinate, the construction and exactness dependency still
2866: accepts the original tuple for $c$, while the extension-budget theorem says that
2867: some required finite coordinate for $c^{+}$ is missing.  The obstruction skeleton
2868: therefore returns the first failed finite field as a bad subrecord.  This is
2869: precisely the maximality assertion: the accepted record is largest among the
2870: finite consequences supported by the submitted tuple, but it does not cross into
2871: any stronger reading whose extension budget is unpaid.  Since all three steps
2872: quantify only over the verifier's finite coordinates, their composite cannot
2873: certify external semantic or implementation facts that are not among those
2874: coordinates.
2875: \end{proof}
2876: 
2877: \begin{proposition}[Gate-interface separation]
2878: \label{prop:gate-interface-separation}
2879: The promotion, overlap, submission-state, and theorem-content delta gates are
2880: separate deterministic interfaces.  None can be replaced by passage of another
2881: one when a load-bearing claim has the corresponding component.
2882: \end{proposition}
2883: 
2884: \begin{proof}
2885: Each named gate has a distinct invariant and evidence surface in the operational
2886: definition.  Promotion asks whether a candidate has crossed the human
2887: publication boundary; its evidence is the active-paper directory and pipeline
2888: metadata.  Overlap asks whether the route duplicates or conflicts with a sibling
2889: route; its evidence is route identity, overlap ledgers, submitted-sibling paths,
2890: and board state.  Submission-state asks whether the proposed publication action
2891: is compatible with submission markers, venue state, and submission-pack metadata.
2892: The theorem-content delta gate asks whether an asserted extension contains
2893: load-bearing theorem growth; its evidence is an inventory, hard-lint surface, or
2894: delta record.  These predicates are logically independent at the interface
2895: level: an active paper can still overlap a submitted sibling, a route with no
2896: overlap can still have an impermissible submission state, and a compiling edit in
2897: a permissible route can still lack theorem-content delta.  Therefore a
2898: load-bearing claim with any one of these components must pass that component's
2899: own gate, and passage through the others cannot supply the missing evidence.\end{proof}
2900: 
2901: \begin{corollary}[Human promotion is not daemon-visible active-track equivalence]
2902: \label{cor:human-promotion-daemon-active-track-separation}
2903: In the operational interface of Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface},
2904: a human-promotion record and daemon-visible active-paper markers are not
2905: interchangeable certificate fields.  More precisely, let $c$ be any
2906: route-boundary claim whose accepted certificate is
2907: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$.  The certificate may be read as saying that a
2908: candidate is admitted to the publication-facing package only when the promotion
2909: field $H$ records the human editorial act and the corresponding route-gate pass
2910: record in $R$ names the active-paper marker surface required by the promotion
2911: gate.  A record containing only a human-promotion note but no active-paper marker
2912: surface does not certify daemon visibility, and a record containing only a
2913: daemon-visible \path{2026_*} marker surface but no human-promotion field does not
2914: certify editorial promotion.  In particular, the intake-isolation row $a_3$ of
2915: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}
2916: supports exactly the bounded lesson that the intake packet remains outside
2917: daemon-visible publication work until both sides of the promotion interface are
2918: recorded; it does not identify human promotion with daemon enumeration.
2919: \end{corollary}
2920: 
2921: \begin{proof}
2922: Clause A4 of Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} assigns the
2923: route-boundary information to the certificate coordinate $H$.  The route-gate
2924: part of Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} is a different
2925: finite coordinate: its pass record belongs to $R$ and its evidence surface is the
2926: active-paper marker surface, namely a human-promotion act producing a
2927: \path{2026_*} directory with \path{main.tex} and \path{PIPELINE.md}.  Thus the
2928: schema checks two typed fields, not one untyped publication-status fact.  By
2929: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, the finite verifier accepts a
2930: route-boundary certificate exactly when all required finite schema predicates
2931: hold.  If the active-paper marker surface is missing, then the route-gate pass
2932: record lacks its required evidence pointer in $R$; by
2933: Theorem~\ref{thm:mixed-claim-projection-obstruction} this is an
2934: inclusion-minimal bad subrecord for the daemon-visible reading.  If the
2935: human-promotion field is missing, then A4 fails for the route-boundary crossing;
2936: this is a distinct missing-coordinate obstruction in $H$.  Proposition~\ref{prop:gate-interface-separation}
2937: prevents repairing either bad subrecord by passage through overlap,
2938: submission-state, scheduler, discovery, artifact, or theorem-content gates.
2939: 
2940: For row $a_3$, the acceptance ledger names the intake seed and active-paper
2941: detector paths, while the certificate ledger records the marker that the intake
2942: seed does not cross into publication work before promotion.  The case-witness
2943: table states the same failed active-paper invariant and detector block.  Applying
2944: the preceding paragraph to these finite coordinates gives the advertised bounded
2945: reading: inactive intake is separated from publication work by the promotion
2946: interface, but the certificate does not collapse the editorial act and daemon
2947: marker enumeration into a single equivalent notion.\end{proof}
2948: 
2949: \begin{proposition}[Scheduler selection is advisory before discovery gating]
2950: \label{prop:scheduler-selection-advisory}
2951: Let $s$ be a scheduler or critical-path score attached to a candidate claim
2952: $c$.  If $s$ recommends selecting $c$, then the recommendation is a
2953: work-selection claim and by itself can only pass the scheduler gate.  If $c$ is
2954: also asserted to be a discovery or classifier-shifting contribution in a
2955: record-gate-sound pipeline, then the accepted mixed claim must pass both the scheduler
2956: gate and the discovery gate.
2957: Consequently, critical-path scores may prioritize review order, and the imported
2958: classifier-shift selection rule may break ties among otherwise equal discovery
2959: potential coordinates, but neither score nor recommendation accepts a
2960: classifier-shift discovery unless the discovery gate also supplies the required
2961: certificate, ledger, and lineage witnesses.
2962: \end{proposition}
2963: 
2964: \begin{proof}
2965: By the definition of $\Gamma$, a scheduler recommendation has claim kind
2966: work-selection, so its atomic gate is the scheduler gate.  The scheduler gate's
2967: evidence surface records a score, backlog window, or analogous critical-path
2968: priority; none of these fields is a semantic naming certificate, a ledger
2969: witness, a DNA-complete theorem genome, a score certificate for a discovery
2970: claim, a lineage witness, or a not-claimed boundary.  Hence scheduler passage
2971: alone records only that $c$ is an eligible or prioritized work item.
2972: 
2973: Now suppose that the same recorded assertion also says that $c$ is a discovery
2974: or classifier-shifting contribution.  Then the assertion is mixed: it has a
2975: work-selection component and a discovery component.  Proposition~\ref{prop:claim-kind-dispatch}
2976: therefore requires passage through every gate in the union assigned by $\Gamma$,
2977: namely the scheduler gate for the work-selection component and the pinned
2978: discovery gate for the discovery component.  The source-interface record in
2979: Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record} includes the imported classifier-change
2980: selection theorem only in its bounded form: with positive classifier-change
2981: weight and all other coordinates fixed, such a selection operator favors the
2982: classifier-changing candidate.  The same source-interface record also includes
2983: pipeline safety, which requires certificate, ledger, genome, score, lineage, and
2984: boundary witnesses for accepted discovery.  Combining these two imported
2985: interfaces gives the stated conclusion: scheduling and classifier-shift scoring
2986: can advise selection order, but discovery acceptance still occurs only through
2987: the discovery gate.\end{proof}
2988: 
2989: The next result is the concrete verifier/interface contribution for the workshop
2990: route.  It constructs a finite certificate from submitted rows, proves that the
2991: certificate is exact for the acceptance relation above, and classifies all
2992: properly stronger readings by the preorder of
2993: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  Its sharpness is the finite
2994: extension-budget normal form of
2995: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, not an
2996: implementation-soundness or semantic-correctness theorem for the underlying
2997: tools.  The load-bearing trust boundary is hypothesis~(2), record-gate
2998: soundness: without the finite predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ tying
2999: ledger entries to assigned passing rows and submitted evidence pointers, the
3000: theorem has no certificate constructor to apply.  The schema-relative exactness
3001: and finite-coordinate classifier of
3002: Theorems~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness},
3003: \ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, and
3004: \ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity} then ensure that the resulting certificate
3005: proves only the finite-record claim it records.  Table~\ref{tab:publication-safety-dependency-map}
3006: separates the dependencies used in this core implication from later
3007: current-package instantiations: rows above ``Current instantiation'' are the
3008: mathematical interface spine, while the current-instantiation row and all later
3009: digest, upload/archive, venue, inventory, and fixed-point labels are support-boundary
3010: applications of the same finite schema.
3011: 
3012: \begin{table}
3013: \caption{Core dependency map and support-instantiation boundary for the finite publication-safety interface}
3014: \label{tab:publication-safety-dependency-map}
3015: \scriptsize
3016: \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{p{0.22\linewidth}p{0.33\linewidth}X}
