3029: 
3030: \begin{proposition}[Minimal bad-record witness for command-run escalation]
3031: \label{prop:publication-safety-minimal-bad-record-witness}
3032: In the operational interface of
3033: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}, let $r$ be a submitted
3034: claim record whose claim-kind set is \{route\}, whose evidence level is declared
3035: to be command-run, and whose boundary field contains no command-run tuple
3036: $(\text{command},\text{source state},\text{environment},\text{exit code},
3037: \text{log path})$.  Suppose all other route coordinates of $r$ are filled:
3038: there is an evidence pointer, a human-promotion marker at the declared boundary,
3039: and pass rows for the promotion, overlap, and submission-state gates.  Then the
3040: schema verifier rejects $r$, and the inclusion-minimal bad subrecord is exactly
3041: the pair consisting of the evidence-level declaration ``command-run'' and the
3042: boundary field missing the five command-run coordinates.  In particular, adding
3043: more prose, advisory scores, route history, or path-verified evidence surfaces
3044: cannot repair the rejected stronger reading unless the missing command-run tuple
3045: is added as a finite coordinate.
3046: \end{proposition}
3047: 
3048: \begin{proof}
3049: For a route claim, the dispatch table in
3050: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} requires the promotion,
3051: overlap, and submission-state gates.  By hypothesis those pass rows and the
3052: route-boundary marker are already present, so clauses A1--A4 and A6 of
3053: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} have no missing route,
3054: evidence-surface, gate-coverage, or promotion coordinate to report.  The only
3055: remaining clause that reads the declared evidence level is the command-run part
3056: of the boundary condition: at command-run strength the boundary field must
3057: contain the command, immutable source state, environment, exit code, and log
3058: path.  These five fields are absent by hypothesis, so the verifier rejects.
3059: 
3060: The bad subrecord is inclusion-minimal.  If the evidence-level declaration is
3061: removed, the command-run boundary obligation is no longer triggered by the
3062: record.  If the boundary field is replaced by one containing the five command-run
3063: coordinates, the displayed obstruction disappears and the record must be tested
3064: against the remaining clauses, which were assumed filled.  Deleting any unrelated
3065: prose, advisory score, route-history note, or path-verified surface does not add
3066: the missing five fields and therefore cannot change the failed command-run
3067: clause.  Thus the rejected stronger reading is witnessed by a finite bad record,
3068: not by a semantic judgment about the surrounding pipeline.  This is the concrete
3069: negative instance used by Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface},
3070: Proposition~\ref{prop:publication-safety-maximality}, and
3071: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} when a path-verified route
3072: or submission row is silently upgraded to command-run strength.
3073: \end{proof}
3074: \begin{theorem}[Bounded software-audit interface under record-gate soundness]
3075: \label{thm:publication-safety-interface}
3076: Consider a theory-to-paper pipeline with the operational interface above and a
3077: finite submitted record surface consisting only of the manuscript source, printed
3078: tables, submitted manifests, uploaded review-bundle records, recorded logs, and
3079: explicitly declared historical boundaries.  Assume the following four distinct
3080: finite-record hypotheses.
3081: \begin{enumerate}
3082: \item \emph{Acceptance entries}: every load-bearing claim in an active manuscript
3083: is either rejected or has an acceptance entry with an evidence surface inside
3084: this finite submitted record surface.
3085: \item \emph{Record-gate soundness}: writing $S$ for this finite submitted record
3086: surface and $L$ for the acceptance ledger, the package satisfies the finite
3087: predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ of
3088: Definitions~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} and
3089: \ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface} as proved equivalent in
3090: Proposition~\ref{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness} and as
3091: delimited by Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}: every recorded
3092: acceptance entry has the assigned passing rows and submitted evidence pointers,
3093: every advisory output is demoted unless the deterministic gate set $\Gamma$ for
3094: its claim kind passes, and no independent implementation-soundness,
3095: fresh-execution, or semantic-correctness claim about the gates is inferred unless
3096: the package also contains the corresponding source path, command, source state,
3097: environment, exit code, and log path.
3098: \item \emph{Discovery certificate input}: discovery claims are checked through
3099: the pinned discovery-gate interface certified by
3100: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} and
3101: Propositions~\ref{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface},
3102: \ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}, and
3103: \ref{prop:source-interface-evidence-level-separation}, in particular the
3104: separated manual/path-verified and executable finite-record levels for the
3105: pipeline safety and main pipeline theorem labels.
3106: \item \emph{Promotion and verifier boundary}: no inactive intake seed is treated
3107: as publication work without human promotion, and the constructed tuple below is
3108: submitted to the finite schema verifier rather than being identified with the
3109: acceptance entry itself.
3110: \end{enumerate}
3111: Then, and only under these finite-record hypotheses, the package checker has the
3112: following bounded software-audit invariant.  The checker input is the submitted surface
3113: $S$, the ledger $L$, the dispatch table $\Gamma$, and the schema files and bundle
3114: records named in Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}.  Its
3115: output is the finite accepted set
3116: \[
3117: \mathsf{Out}_{\Gamma,S}(L)=
3118: \{(c,\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)):\ V(c,\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c))=1\}
3119: \]
3120: together with the finite list of rejected bad subrecords.  For every accepted
3121: load-bearing claim $c$ it constructs, from the acceptance entry and the submitted
3122: finite evidence surfaces, a record
3123: \[
3124: \mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)=\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)
3125: \]
3126: accepted by the finite schema verifier of
3127: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} with the input--predicate--output
3128: boundary of Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}; equivalently, by the
3129: schema-relative finite-record exactness of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, the
3130: constructed record satisfies the schema-relative record-acceptance semantics for
3131: the dispatch table $\Gamma$.  Consequently, within the submitted finite records
3132: and under the stated explicit record-gate-soundness hypothesis, $c$ has a recorded evidence
3133: surface, a declared evidence boundary, and a prior pass record for every gate
3134: required for its claim kind.  Accepted discovery claims additionally inherit the
3135: bounded pinned source-interface consequences that statement-only, code-only,
3136: mechanical-expansion, and compression-only outputs are not accepted as discovery
3137: without the required certificate, ledger, and lineage witnesses.  Moreover, the constructed record is axis-and-level invariant and sharp for the
3138: stronger-reading preorder.  If $c\preceq_{\Gamma}c^{+}$ is a properly stronger
3139: reading that changes architecture axis, raises evidence level, adds an
3140: implementation, artifact, venue, or semantic-correctness assertion, or adds a
3141: theorem-obligation completeness assertion, then $c^{+}$ is accepted by the same
3142: interface if and only if the submitted record surface contains the corresponding
3143: finite extension coordinates and those records pass the assigned gates.  If those
3144: coordinates are absent, the accepted certificate is maximal below $c^{+}$: it
3145: still accepts $c$ and rejects the stronger reading by a finite bad subrecord.
3146: \end{theorem}
3147: 
3148: \begin{proof}
3149: We prove the theorem directly from the finite schema.  The first hypothesis gives
3150: one ledger entry for each accepted load-bearing claim and places its evidence
3151: surface inside the finite submitted record surface $S$.  The record-gate
3152: soundness hypothesis says precisely that this entry has the passing rows assigned
3153: by the dispatch table $\Gamma$ and that advisory agent output is demoted unless
3154: those rows are present.  The discovery-input hypothesis supplies the only extra
3155: coordinates required for discovery-kind claims: the pinned source-interface rows,
3156: lineage rows, and evidence-level separation rows.  The promotion hypothesis
3157: separates active publication claims from inactive intake seeds, and the verifier
3158: boundary says that the object checked next is the tuple
3159: \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), not an informal acceptance note.
3160: 
3161: For an accepted claim $c$, construct $K$ from its claim-kind field, $\ell$ from
3162: its declared evidence level, $E$ from its finite evidence surface, $H$ from the
3163: human-promotion field, $R$ from the gate-pass rows assigned by $\Gamma$, $D$ from
3164: any pinned discovery rows required by the claim kind, and $B$ from the
3165: command-boundary tuple.  These are exactly the coordinates read by the verifier
3166: input contract of Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}.  The six
3167: acceptance predicates of Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}
3168: therefore hold: the claim kind is in the finite domain, the evidence level is one
3169: of the schema levels, each assigned gate has a pass row and evidence pointer,
3170: promotion is present exactly when a route crossing is asserted, discovery rows
3171: are present exactly for discovery-kind claims, and command-run strength is used
3172: only with command, source-state, environment, exit-code, and log-path fields.
3173: By Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, verifier acceptance is
3174: equivalent to schema-relative record acceptance for this same tuple.  This proves
3175: that the map
3176: \[
3177: (S,L,\Gamma,V)\longmapsto
3178: \bigl(\mathsf{Out}_{\Gamma,S}(L),\mathsf{Bad}_{\Gamma,S}(L)\bigr)
3179: \]
3180: is a finite record function of the submitted surface, ledger, dispatch table,
3181: and schema verifier.
3182: 
3183: It remains to prove the sharp stronger-reading clause.  The preorder
3184: $c\preceq_{\Gamma}c^{+}$ can strengthen a claim only by changing a finite field
3185: read by the schema or by adding a finite coordinate assigned by $\Gamma$: an
3186: architecture-axis tag, an evidence-level tag, an implementation or semantic tag,
3187: a venue/support tag, a theorem-obligation tag, or a command-run tag.  Sufficiency
3188: is immediate from the same six predicates: if the corresponding extension
3189: coordinate is present and its assigned gate rows pass, the enlarged tuple again
3190: satisfies the finite schema and is accepted.  Necessity follows by projecting to
3191: the first changed finite field.  Without the required extension coordinate, two
3192: submitted states can agree on every field read by the current certificate for
3193: $c$ and differ only on the asserted stronger fact; the verifier has no input
3194: coordinate on which to accept that stronger fact.  The failed tag together with
3195: its missing coordinate is a finite bad subrecord, and deleting any unrelated
3196: fields leaves the same failure.  Thus the original certificate is maximal below
3197: $c^{+}$ and the rejection is inclusion-minimal.
3198: 
3199: The proof is schema-relative.  It establishes finite publication certificates
3200: and finite bad-subrecord reports under the declared record-gate predicate only.